1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,095
You send a picture to a friend. It appears as
one

2
00:00:05,095 --> 00:00:10,190
complete image on your screen, so perhaps the
Internet carries it

3
00:00:10,190 --> 00:00:15,286
as one big object. Here are two computers and
a connection

4
00:00:15,286 --> 00:00:20,381
between them. Connected devices that exchange
information form a network. We

5
00:00:20,381 --> 00:00:25,476
call a connection along the way a link. The
computers send

6
00:00:25,476 --> 00:00:30,571
data: information represented electronically.
Our blue rectangle stands for the picture's

7
00:00:30,571 --> 00:00:35,667
data. How should it take its turn on that
link?

8
00:00:35,667 --> 00:00:40,314
Instead of sending that whole blue rectangle
as one uninterrupted unit,

9
00:00:40,314 --> 00:00:44,962
divide its data into smaller pieces. Add a
little handling information

10
00:00:44,962 --> 00:00:49,610
to each, including where it needs to go. That
unit is

11
00:00:49,610 --> 00:00:54,257
called a packet. Think of several labeled
parcels instead of one

12
00:00:54,257 --> 00:00:58,905
enormous delivery. The numbers in our drawing
help us follow the

13
00:00:58,905 --> 00:01:03,552
pieces; they are not a claim that every
network packet has

14
00:01:03,552 --> 00:01:08,200
a sequence number.

15
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:13,295
Now your friend sends a tiny green message
while the blue

16
00:01:13,295 --> 00:01:18,390
picture is being sent. If the entire picture
took one uninterrupted

17
00:01:18,390 --> 00:01:23,486
turn, green would wait for the whole thing.
With packets, there

18
00:01:23,486 --> 00:01:28,581
is a choice between pieces. Send one blue
packet, then green,

19
00:01:28,581 --> 00:01:33,676
then more blue. The link still sends one
stream of data,

20
00:01:33,676 --> 00:01:38,771
but several conversations can share it.
Dividing data creates chances to

21
00:01:38,771 --> 00:01:43,867
share; it does not guarantee every message an
immediate turn.

22
00:01:43,867 --> 00:01:48,390
Zoom inside one packet. Computers represent
information using zeros and ones,

23
00:01:48,390 --> 00:01:52,914
called bits. Those bits take time to enter a
link. A

24
00:01:52,914 --> 00:01:57,438
link's bit rate is how many it can send each
second.

25
00:01:57,438 --> 00:02:01,962
So two things affect a turn: how many bits the
packet

26
00:02:01,962 --> 00:02:06,486
contains, and how many bits the link sends per
second. More

27
00:02:06,486 --> 00:02:11,010
bits mean a longer turn. A higher rate means a
shorter

28
00:02:11,010 --> 00:02:15,533
one.

29
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:20,325
A byte contains eight bits. Our example packet
has fifteen hundred

30
00:02:20,325 --> 00:02:25,117
bytes, so multiply by eight to get twelve
thousand bits. The

31
00:02:25,117 --> 00:02:29,908
link sends ten million bits each second.
Divide twelve thousand by

32
00:02:29,908 --> 00:02:34,700
ten million and we get zero point zero zero
one two

33
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:39,492
seconds: one point two milliseconds. A
millisecond is one thousandth of

34
00:02:39,492 --> 00:02:44,283
a second. The shorthand is L divided by R:
length in

35
00:02:44,283 --> 00:02:49,075
bits divided by rate. This measures putting
data onto the link;

36
00:02:49,075 --> 00:02:53,867
signal travel time is extra.

37
00:02:53,867 --> 00:02:58,833
Real journeys can pass through devices between
the two computers. Add

38
00:02:58,833 --> 00:03:03,800
two forwarding devices, labeled switches, that
pass data to the next

39
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:08,767
link. In our model, each waits until a whole
packet arrives

40
00:03:08,767 --> 00:03:13,733
before sending that packet onward. We call
this store-and-forward. The name

41
00:03:13,733 --> 00:03:18,700
describes the action: receive it, then pass it
on. Watch packet

42
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:23,667
one finish the first step before starting the
second. We will

43
00:03:23,667 --> 00:03:28,633
keep this path and rule for our experiment.

44
00:03:28,633 --> 00:03:33,367
Our path now has three links of equal rate.
One packet

45
00:03:33,367 --> 00:03:38,100
spends one point two milliseconds being sent
on the first, then

46
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:42,833
the second, then the third. Add those three
turns: three point

47
00:03:42,833 --> 00:03:47,567
six milliseconds. For this little experiment,
we ignore travel time, device

48
00:03:47,567 --> 00:03:52,300
processing, and any other traffic. Those
things matter in real life,

49
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:57,033
but hiding them briefly lets us see the
pattern. With only

50
00:03:57,033 --> 00:04:01,767
one packet, the steps happen in order. What
happens if we

51
00:04:01,767 --> 00:04:06,500
have several packets ready?

52
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:11,292
Packet one has reached the first switch. It is
now being

53
00:04:11,292 --> 00:04:16,083
sent on link two. Look back at link one: it is

54
00:04:16,083 --> 00:04:20,875
free. Why leave it doing nothing? Send packet
two there. A

55
00:04:20,875 --> 00:04:25,667
moment later, packet one uses link three,
packet two uses link

56
00:04:25,667 --> 00:04:30,458
two, and packet three can use link one.
Different parts of

57
00:04:30,458 --> 00:04:35,250
the path work at once. This is called a
pipeline, like

58
00:04:35,250 --> 00:04:40,042
several parcels moving through different
stations. Each parcel still visits every

59
00:04:40,042 --> 00:04:44,833
station in the proper order.

60
00:04:44,833 --> 00:04:49,800
Let's draw that motion as a timetable. Each
row is one

61
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:54,767
link. Each column is one turn of one point two
milliseconds.

62
00:04:54,767 --> 00:04:59,733
A colored box shows which packet uses that
link during that

63
00:04:59,733 --> 00:05:04,700
turn. First, only the top row works. Next, two
rows work.

64
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:09,667
Then all three do. Follow one color
diagonally: that is one

65
00:05:09,667 --> 00:05:14,633
packet moving through its three steps. Follow
one column downward: those

66
00:05:14,633 --> 00:05:19,600
are different packets moving at the same time.

67
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:24,695
Continue until all four packets arrive. The
first packet takes three

68
00:05:24,695 --> 00:05:29,790
turns to reach the end. After that, another
packet finishes each

69
00:05:29,790 --> 00:05:34,886
turn. There are three more packets, so add
three more turns:

70
00:05:34,886 --> 00:05:39,981
six in total. Six times one point two
milliseconds gives seven

71
00:05:39,981 --> 00:05:45,076
point two milliseconds. If we waited for each
packet's whole journey

72
00:05:45,076 --> 00:05:50,171
before starting the next, we would need twelve
turns. We simply

73
00:05:50,171 --> 00:05:55,267
let separate links do useful work at the same
time.

74
00:05:55,267 --> 00:06:00,000
Now the formula is just a short version of our
counting.

75
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,733
Call the number of links H and the number of
packets

76
00:06:04,733 --> 00:06:09,467
N. The first packet needs H turns. The
remaining N minus

77
00:06:09,467 --> 00:06:14,200
one packets each add a turn. Together, that is
H plus

78
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:18,933
N minus one. Multiply by the time for one
turn, L

79
00:06:18,933 --> 00:06:23,667
divided by R. That gives our total. It works
for this

80
00:06:23,667 --> 00:06:28,400
clean model with equal packets and equal-rate
links, without extra travel,

81
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:33,133
processing, or traffic delays.

82
00:06:33,133 --> 00:06:38,229
Bring back the green message. Real
conversations are uneven. Someone sends

83
00:06:38,229 --> 00:06:43,324
a picture, another person pauses, then a new
message arrives. Packet

84
00:06:43,324 --> 00:06:48,419
switching means the network handles and
forwards individual packets, allowing shared

85
00:06:48,419 --> 00:06:53,514
links to serve different conversations. A
quiet sender need not keep

86
00:06:53,514 --> 00:06:58,610
occupying a turn. A ready packet can use the
next available

87
00:06:58,610 --> 00:07:03,705
opportunity when selected. Two useful effects:
different conversations can share one

88
00:07:03,705 --> 00:07:08,800
link, and different links can work on
different pieces together.

89
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:13,646
There is a limit. If packets arrive at a
device faster

90
00:07:13,646 --> 00:07:18,492
than its outgoing link can send them, they
form a waiting

91
00:07:18,492 --> 00:07:23,338
line, called a queue. Green can still get
stuck behind blue.

92
00:07:23,338 --> 00:07:28,183
Splitting the picture does not create an
endlessly fast connection or

93
00:07:28,183 --> 00:07:33,029
a perfectly fair order. If the waiting space
fills, a packet

94
00:07:33,029 --> 00:07:37,875
may even be discarded. So our claim is modest
and useful:

95
00:07:37,875 --> 00:07:42,721
packet boundaries give the network manageable
turns to share, while actual

96
00:07:42,721 --> 00:07:47,567
capacity and queue choices still matter.

97
00:07:47,567 --> 00:07:52,358
Why not make every piece tiny? Short turns
sound wonderful. But

98
00:07:52,358 --> 00:07:57,150
remember the handling information added to
each packet. That information uses

99
00:07:57,150 --> 00:08:01,942
space too. Blue is useful picture data; yellow
is the extra

100
00:08:01,942 --> 00:08:06,733
label, called a header. Divide the same blue
data into more

101
00:08:06,733 --> 00:08:11,525
pieces and count the yellow labels. There are
more of them.

102
00:08:11,525 --> 00:08:16,317
Devices also have more individual packets to
handle. Smaller pieces can

103
00:08:16,317 --> 00:08:21,108
help sharing, but they are not free. We must
balance shorter

104
00:08:21,108 --> 00:08:25,900
turns against this extra work.

105
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:30,579
At the destination, the blue pieces belong to
the picture and

106
00:08:30,579 --> 00:08:35,258
green belongs to the little message. The
receiving computer uses the

107
00:08:35,258 --> 00:08:39,938
arriving data to recover the content the
application needs. Keeping things

108
00:08:39,938 --> 00:08:44,617
complete and in order involves other parts of
the communication system;

109
00:08:44,617 --> 00:08:49,296
packet switching alone does not promise that.
Here, all our pieces

110
00:08:49,296 --> 00:08:53,975
arrive successfully. Notice the useful trick:
the network could move each

111
00:08:53,975 --> 00:08:58,654
piece along without giving the entire picture
one enormous turn from

112
00:08:58,654 --> 00:09:03,333
beginning to end.

113
00:09:03,333 --> 00:09:08,179
We began with one picture on a screen.
Breaking its data

114
00:09:08,179 --> 00:09:13,025
into packets gave us shorter turns on a shared
link. Those

115
00:09:13,025 --> 00:09:17,871
pieces also let several links work at once,
just like the

116
00:09:17,871 --> 00:09:22,717
stations in our parcel line. We still pay for
headers, we

117
00:09:22,717 --> 00:09:27,562
can still wait in queues, and every link has a
limit.

118
00:09:27,562 --> 00:09:32,408
But now a big picture and a tiny message can
use

119
00:09:32,408 --> 00:09:37,254
the same network in manageable pieces. The
Internet's useful trick starts

120
00:09:37,254 --> 00:09:42,100
with something simple: divide the work.
